course: world
lesson: 35
title: Teeth and their care
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Teeth cut, tear and grind food so it can be swallowed and digested.
explain: Children have 20 milk (baby) teeth. From about age 6 they fall out one by one and are replaced by adult teeth; an adult has 32 teeth.
explain: There are different teeth for different jobs: sharp incisors at the front cut, pointed canines tear, and wide molars at the back grind.
explain: Each tooth is covered by enamel, the hardest material in the body. But germs in the mouth eat sugar left on the teeth and make acid, and the acid makes holes called cavities.
explain: Brushing twice a day with fluoride toothpaste, eating fewer sugary snacks and visiting the dentist keep teeth healthy. A grown-up tooth that is lost does not grow back.
fact: Children have 20 milk teeth.
fact: An adult has 32 teeth.
fact: Milk teeth begin to fall out at around age 6 and adult teeth grow in their place.
fact: Incisors at the front cut food.
fact: Canines are pointed teeth for tearing food.
fact: Molars at the back grind and crush food.
fact: Enamel, the outer layer of a tooth, is the hardest material in the body.
fact: Germs in the mouth turn sugar into acid that damages enamel.
fact: A hole in a tooth caused by decay is called a cavity.
fact: Fluoride in toothpaste helps make enamel stronger.
fact: We should brush our teeth twice a day for about two minutes.
fact: Adult teeth do not grow back if they are lost.
exercise: How many milk teeth does a child have? | 20 | 32 | 10 | 46
exercise: How many teeth does an adult usually have? | 32 | 20 | 24 | 50
exercise: Which teeth at the front are used for cutting? | Incisors | Molars | Canines | Wisdom teeth
exercise: Which teeth at the back grind food? | Molars | Incisors | Canines | Milk teeth
exercise: What are the pointed teeth for tearing called? | Canines | Molars | Incisors | Gums
exercise: What is the hardest material in the human body? | Tooth enamel | The thigh bone | The skull | Fingernails
exercise: What makes holes in teeth? | Acid made by germs feeding on sugar | Drinking too much water every single day | Chewing hard vegetables like raw carrots | Brushing them twice a day with toothpaste
exercise: What is a cavity? | A hole in a tooth caused by decay | A new tooth growing in | A kind of toothpaste | The root of a tooth
exercise: What does fluoride in toothpaste do? | It makes enamel stronger | It feeds the germs | It whitens the gums | It makes teeth grow longer
exercise: How often should we brush our teeth? | Twice a day | Once a week | Only when they hurt | After every sip of water
exercise: At about what age do milk teeth start to fall out? | About 6 | About 1 | About 15 | About 25
exercise: What happens if an adult loses a tooth? | It does not grow back | A new one grows within a month | A milk tooth grows in its place | It grows back if you brush more
exercise: Why do we have teeth of different shapes? | Each shape does a different job on food | Different shapes look nicer when we smile | Only one shape is used and the rest are spare | Shapes change with the weather
exercise: Who is the doctor who looks after teeth? | A dentist | A vet | An optician | A baker
test: A boy sips a sweet fizzy drink all afternoon. Why is this bad for his teeth? | The germs keep making acid from the sugar all afternoon | The bubbles crack the enamel | Cold drinks make teeth fall out | The drink fills his cavities with gas
test: When you bite into an apple, which teeth do most of the cutting? | The incisors at the front | The molars at the very back of the mouth | The roots under the gum | The wisdom teeth only
test: Which is true? | Adult teeth must last for life, because they do not grow back | Every lost tooth grows back after a year | Milk teeth never need brushing | Enamel can feel pain like skin
test: A girl chews a nut into a paste. Which teeth did the grinding? | The molars | The incisors | The canines | The milk gums
test: What would help teeth most after eating sweets? | Brushing them with fluoride toothpaste | Eating more sweets to cover the old sugar | Drinking a sweet juice | Biting on ice to harden them
test: Why do dentists say "fewer sugary snacks"? | Germs make acid from sugar, and every snack feeds them again | Sugar makes teeth grow too big | Sugar turns enamel into bone | Snacks stop saliva for a week
test: A 7-year-old has a wobbly front tooth. What is most likely happening? | A milk tooth is being replaced by an adult tooth | She will have no teeth there forever | The tooth has turned into a molar | It means her bones are breaking
test: What protects the inside of a tooth? | A hard layer of enamel | A thin layer of soft skin over the top | A coat of saliva only | A thin layer of hair
test: why do my back teeth look all flat n bumpy | They are molars, made to grind and crush food | They are canines, worn flat by tearing | They are milk teeth that never grew | They are incisors used for cutting
test: sweets dont even touch the tooth inside so how do they rot it | Germs turn the sugar into acid that eats the enamel | Sugar is sharp and scratches the teeth | Sweets make the teeth soft and wet | Sugar freezes the teeth and cracks them
test: my cousin says if u lose an adult tooth another one grows back. true? | No, adult teeth do not grow back | Yes, teeth always grow back every time | Yes, but only the front teeth grow back | No, only the molars grow back, not the rest
test: Ella reckons brushing once a week is fine. right? | No, brush twice a day for about two minutes | Yes, once a week is enough | Yes, if you use lots of toothpaste | No, brush once a month but for much longer each time
exam: State the number of milk teeth possessed by a child. | Twenty | Thirty-two | Twenty-eight | Twelve
exam: Identify the teeth whose function is the grinding and crushing of food. | The molars | The incisors | The canines | The milk teeth alone
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises enamel? | It is the hardest material in the human body | It is the softest layer of the tooth | It is a living tissue that regrows when it is chipped | It is a substance contained in toothpaste
exam: Identify the principal cause of damage to the enamel of the teeth. | Acid produced from sugar by germs in the mouth | Excessive brushing with fluoride | The drinking of plain water | The chewing of raw vegetables
